A listing is a picture drawn once for the whole film and held to wherever its subject appears, so name only what the telling returns to: a person, place or thing the film must show more than once — again in a later chapter, or more than once across the shots of the chapter it lives in — and must show looking the same each time. A passer-by, a room one shot passes through, an object a single shot holds and the telling never comes back to is drawn by that shot and its words, and earns no entry of its own: leave it out of all three lists, however sharply the telling draws it.
Describe each one as a picture would have to know it: for a character, only what a drawing can show — age, build, face and hair, clothes, and any mark or accessory they wear; for a scene, its size, its light and the state it is in; for a prop, its shape, its material and its wear. A character's entry is a look and nothing else: no voice or way of speaking, no habits, mood or manner, no relationships, no backstory, and nothing that happens in the telling.
Each character is drawn on its own, one person at a time, so a character is one person and its description describes that one person: a couple, a pair, a family or any other two or more the telling follows are that many characters, each listed and described on their own. A crowd, a queue or any other group the telling never follows person by person is not a character — keep it out of the cast, and let the scene it stands in say it is there.
Each scene is drawn on its own as the place a shot stands in, one place to an entry, and an entry is one concrete place rather than the telling's words for several places: two people each at their own workplace are two scenes, one for each place, and the one workplace they stand in together is still the one. A platform and the carriage it opens onto are two scenes, and the same place at another hour or in other weather is still the one place, with the change written into its description.
Each prop is drawn on its own too, and only earns a listing where the story turns on it — what is handed over, hidden, found, carried or lost — and the telling comes back to it enough that its look has to stay. Light, shadow, weather, a moon, a sky and the ordinary fittings of a place are not props: they are the scene's light and state, and the scene's description says them. Leave out anything no shot would have to show, and anything only one shot shows.
